description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:353-371. The dedup loop is:

    for entry in &relevant {
        for id in &entry.created {
            if !destroyed.contains(id) && !created.contains(id) { ... }
        }
        for id in &entry.updated {
            if !destroyed.contains(id) && !created.contains(id) && !updated.contains(id) { ... }
        }
        for id in &entry.destroyed {
            created.retain(|c| c != id);
            updated.retain(|u| u != id);
            if !destroyed.contains(id) { ... }
        }
    }

Complexity class: Vec::contains is O(n). For relevant.len() == K entries each with C ids, this is O(K * C * (created_so_far + destroyed_so_far + updated_so_far)). Worst case O(K^2 * C^2). The retain calls add another linear factor.

Trigger scale: an account with sustained mutation that has not been compacted has a change_log entries grow to thousands. A /changes call spanning all of it walks the entire list and the dedup is N^2 over total ids.

The fix is the standard one: build three HashSets (created_set, updated_set, destroyed_set) parallel to the Vecs and use them for contains/retain. Memory cost is bounded by the same N — no real downside.

This is in the reference impl path and is documented as not-production, BUT downstream implementors copying this shape will inherit the quadratic behavior. The shape appears in 6 sibling MemoryBackends — propagation needed.
